  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThrough Sunday...High pressure shifting east allows for southwest \nreturn flow to kick in. Net result will be temperatures not as \ncold tonight as they were last night, with minimal chance for any\nfrost/freeze. Winds will be gusty, especially this afternoon and \non Sunday afternoon, with gusts up to 30 mph. High temperatures a \nbit below normal today (normal highs are in the mid to upper 50s \nnow), but rebounding into the lower 60s on Sunday.\n\nNext Week...High pressure and mid-level ridging will remain in\nplace for the first half of the work week, keeping conditions\ndry. LREF consistent showing small chances (20-40%) for light QPF\nlate Wednesday into Wednesday night. Chances for more than 0.10 \ninch are minimal though. NBM probabilities for any precip still\nlow though, less than 10%. Overall pattern this week will be dry\nwith a warming trend. Highs early this week start off in the low\nto mid 60s, but warm to near 70 by late this week into next weekend.\nLow temperatures will mostly be in the mid 30s to lower 40s early\nthis week, warming to the 40s and even lower 50s later this week. \nWinds will be light most of the week, but potential for SW winds \nup to 20 mph increases Friday into Saturday ahead of stronger low \npressure system over the plains. \n\nDue to recent rainfall, fine fuels are projected to be only \nmarginally receptive to risk of wildfires even with the warmer \ntemperatures. Will have to keep eye on this though given the \nincreasing amount of dead fine fuels with recent sub freezing \ntemps.",
